<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations Charter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Charter is the founding treaty of the United Nations, signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 26 June 1945 by 50 states and in force from 24 October 1945. It sets out the organisation's purposes, above all the maintenance of international peace and security, and establishes its main bodies, including the General Assembly, the Security Council and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a> is credited with drafting its preamble.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter.jpg" alt="United Nations Charter" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Nations. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uncharter.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The framework of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> was worked out in a series of wartime conferences. The Declaration of St James's Palace, issued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 12 June 1941, was the first joint statement of the aims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. About two months later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom issued the Atlantic Charter, many of whose principles inspired the Charter. On 1 January 1942 representatives of thirty nations at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> signed the Declaration by United Nations. The Declaration of the Four Nations, signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 30 October 1943, was the first formal announcement that a general international organisation was contemplated to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>.</p><p>From 21 August to 7 October 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> hosted the Dumbarton Oaks Conference, where many of the Charter's provisions were proposed, including the Security Council. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalta-conference">Yalta Conference</a> of February 1945 settled the debate over the Council's voting structure and called a conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The San Francisco Conference opened on 25 April 1945. About 850 delegates attended, with advisers and organisers making 3,500 participants, and committees held more than 400 meetings. After a final full meeting on 25 June the text was unanimously approved, and it was signed on 26 June 1945 by 50 of the 51 original member countries.</p><p>The Charter consists of a preamble and 111 articles in 19 chapters. Chapter I states the purposes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, above all the maintenance of international peace and security. Chapters III to XV set out the principal organs: the General Assembly, the Security Council, the Economic and Social Council, the Trusteeship Council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> and the Secretariat. Chapter VII lets the Security Council impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> or authorise the use of force to deal with threats to the peace. Article 103 gives the Charter's obligations precedence over those of other agreements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Charter entered into force on 24 October 1945, after ratification by the five permanent members of the Security Council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the United Kingdom, and a majority of the other signatories. That date is considered the official start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The first session of the General Assembly, with all 51 original members, opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the following January. Amendments need a two-thirds vote of the Assembly and ratification by two-thirds of member states, including all the permanent members of the Council. It is an important part of public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charter of the United Nations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Internationally, Smuts helped shape the League of Nations and is credited with drafting the preamble to the <b>United Nations Charter</b> in 1945, yet the Union’s refusal to place South West Africa under UN trusteeship and its racial laws made it a target of growing criticism at the United…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>United Nations Charter</b> entered into force once a majority of signatories, including the five permanent Security Council members, had ratified it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-24">24 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
